<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul I Šubić of Bribir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1245–1312</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul I Šubić of Bribir was a Croatian nobleman who became ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> in the 1270s and the most powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> of the kingdom. Ruling much of Croatia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> almost independently, he styled himself lord of Bosnia and backed the Angevin Charles Robert for the Hungarian crown. His family's power declined soon after his death in 1312.</p></div>
